package teyru

// Threads, shaped like java.lang.Thread.
//
// The C half lives in internal/runtime/src/tyrt_thread.c: a Teyru thread is an
// operating system thread, the runtime keeps one registry entry per thread, and
// the collector stops every one of them before it traces the heap. This file is
// the part a program writes against: a Thread object with a Runnable to run,
// and the handful of methods java.lang.Thread has for asking about it.
//
// What is implemented:
//
//   - Thread(Runnable), Thread(Runnable, String), Thread() and Thread(String);
//   - start(), which creates the thread and runs run() on it. Calling run()
//     directly runs it on the calling thread, as it does in Java; calling
//     start() twice throws IllegalStateException (Java throws
//     IllegalThreadStateException, and so does this after start());
//   - join(), join(long) is not here -- join() waits for as long as the thread
//     takes, and there is no form that gives up after a while;
//   - isAlive(), getId(), getName(), setName(String);
//   - interrupt(), isInterrupted(), Thread.interrupted(). A thread asleep in
//     Thread.sleep, Thread.join or Object.wait wakes with
//     InterruptedException, and the interrupt status is cleared when it is
//     thrown. Blocking file and socket I/O is not interruptible, which is
//     classic java.io's behaviour too;
//   - setDaemon(boolean) and isDaemon(). A thread inherits its creator's daemon
//     status at construction, as in Java, and the process ends when the last
//     non-daemon thread does -- a daemon that is still looping is simply left
//     behind;
//   - ThreadLocal, in this file, with a per-Thread map. It is deliberately
//     Teyru and not runtime state: the map is an ordinary reference the
//     collector already understands, and a thread's values go away with its
//     Thread object;
//   - Thread.sleep(long), Thread.yield(), Thread.currentThread().
//
//   - Virtual threads, as Java 21 has them: Thread.ofVirtual() and
//     Thread.startVirtualThread(Runnable) start a thread that is multiplexed
//     onto a small pool of carrier threads instead of owning an operating
//     system thread, and Thread.isVirtual() tells the two apart. A virtual
//     thread parks -- gives its carrier up -- in Thread.sleep, Thread.join,
//     Object.wait, and a blocking socket or file read, write, accept or
//     connect. The runtime side is in tyrt_thread.c. On a platform without
//     continuations (Windows) ofVirtual().start() throws
//     UnsupportedOperationException rather than quietly running the work on a
//     platform thread.
//
// Not implemented, and deliberately not declared: the thread priorities,
// ThreadGroup, the uncaught exception handler (the runtime prints the same line
// Java's default handler prints, and the thread ends), Thread.State, and the
// constructors that take a ThreadGroup or a stack size.
//
// The one place this differs from Java in behaviour rather than in surface:
// getId() is assigned when the Thread object is built and never changes, and the
// main thread is 1. Both are Java's rule; the numbers simply are not the JVM's.
// Interrupt status lives on the Thread object, as in Java, so a thread that was
// interrupted before start() runs with the status set, and one that finished
// keeps whatever status it ended with.

class Thread implements Runnable {
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ natives
  //
  // Each is the C entry point of the same name without the 0 suffix, except
  // where the C name would collide with a name this class already has. They are
  // declared here, before anything calls them, because a method has to be
  // declared before it is called.

  /* Start a thread that runs t.run(), and answer with the id it was given. The
     runtime is handed the object rather than a function because the call it has
     to make is the program's own run(), dispatched the way the compiler
     dispatches every other interface call. The daemon flag is passed here
     rather than stored in the runtime earlier: the registry entry does not
     exist until start(), and setDaemon may not be called after it. */
  private static native long start0(Thread t, long id, String name, boolean daemon)

  /* Start a virtual thread that runs t.run(). The runtime makes a continuation
     with its own stack and hands it to a carrier; no operating system thread is
     created. The id is the one the Thread object was given when it was built. */
  private static native void startVirtual0(Thread t, long id, String name)

  /* The id a Thread object gets when it is built: the runtime's counter, so that
     getId() answers the same number before and after start(). */
  private static native long nextId0()

  /* The Thread object of the calling thread, or null when no object has been
     made for it yet -- which for the main thread is exactly once, the first time
     something calls currentThread(). */
  private static native Object current0()
  private static native long currentId0()

  /* Attach this object to the calling thread, so that every later
     currentThread() answers with it. Used for the main thread, which no start()
     ever made an object for. */
  private static native void bind0(Thread t)

  /* Wait for the thread, answering true when the wait was interrupted instead
     of the thread finishing. The status is already cleared when it answers
     true; join() is what turns it into InterruptedException. */
  private static native boolean join0(long id)
  private static native boolean alive0(long id)

  /* Sleep, answering true when the sleep was interrupted (and clearing the
     status). The native call has to be the thing that waits, because it is the
     only layer that can be woken by interrupt0. */
  private static native boolean sleep0(long millis)

  /* Interrupt status, by thread id: set, read, and read-and-clear. The three
     are separate because isInterrupted() must not clear and Thread.interrupted()
     must. */
  private static native void interrupt0(long id)
  private static native boolean isInterrupted0(long id)
  private static native boolean clearInterrupted0()

  /* The calling thread's daemon status, which a new Thread inherits. */
  private static native boolean currentDaemon0()

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------- fields

  private Runnable target
  private String name
  /* The id, whether or not the thread has been started: java.lang.Thread gives
     every thread a number at construction, and a program may read it then. */
  private long tid
  private boolean started
  /* Daemon status, set before start() and read by the runtime from the registry
     entry afterwards. The field is what isDaemon answers for a thread that has
     not started, and what start0 hands the runtime. */
  private boolean daemon
  /* Whether this thread is a virtual one. Set by the builder before start();
     it is what start() dispatches on and what isVirtual() answers. */
  private boolean virtual
  /* This thread's ThreadLocal values, keyed by the ThreadLocal instance. Only
     the thread it belongs to touches it, so it needs no monitor, and it is an
     ordinary reference field so that the collector traces it like any other.
     Package private so that ThreadLocal.remove can test it without making an
     empty map just to throw it away. */
  HashMap<Object, Object> locals
  /* The Thread object standing for the main thread, made on first use. It is a
     static reference, so it is a root the collector keeps: a thread's own object
     must not be collected while the program can still ask about it. */
  private static Thread mainOf

  // ----------------------------------------------------------- constructors

  public Thread() {
    this(null, null)
  }

  public Thread(Runnable target) {
    this(target, null)
  }

  public Thread(String name) {
    this(null, name)
  }

  public Thread(Runnable target, String name) {
    this.tid = Thread.nextId0()
    this.target = target
    this.name = (name == null) ? ("Thread-" + this.tid) : name
    /* java.lang.Thread inherits the daemon status of the thread that makes it,
       which is what keeps a worker created by a daemon a daemon. */
    this.daemon = Thread.currentDaemon0()
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------------- the thread

  /* What the thread runs. A Thread built with a Runnable runs that Runnable's
     run(); a subclass of Thread that overrides run() runs its own, because the
     runtime dispatches this method and not the target. */
  public void run() {
    if (this.target != null) {
      this.target.run()
    }
  }

  /* Run it. The thread that comes back is a real one: it allocates, it can take
     monitors, and the collector scans its stack like any other thread's. */
  public void start() {
    if (this.started) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("thread already started")
    }
    this.started = true
    if (this.virtual) {
      Thread.startVirtual0(this, this.tid, this.name)
    } else {
      Thread.start0(this, this.tid, this.name, this.daemon)
    }
  }

  /* Whether this is a virtual thread. The main thread and every thread built
     with `new Thread(...)` answer false; one built through ofVirtual() answers
     true. */
  public boolean isVirtual() {
    return this.virtual
  }

  /* Wait for this thread to finish. A thread that was never started has nothing
     to wait for, and one that has already finished returns at once -- both are
     what java.lang.Thread.join does. An interrupt of the thread doing the
     joining ends the wait with InterruptedException, and the status is cleared
     as it is thrown. */
  public void join() {
    if (Thread.join0(this.tid)) {
      throw new InterruptedException()
    }
  }

  public boolean isAlive() {
    return Thread.alive0(this.tid)
  }

  public long getId() {
    return this.tid
  }

  public String getName() {
    return this.name
  }

  public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name
  }

  /* Ask this thread to stop waiting. The status is set even when the thread is
     running rather than blocked -- that is the cooperative half of Java's
     interrupt -- and a thread asleep in sleep, join or Object.wait wakes with
     InterruptedException. A thread that has finished, or was never started, is
     not affected. */
  public void interrupt() {
    Thread.interrupt0(this.tid)
  }

  public boolean isInterrupted() {
    return Thread.isInterrupted0(this.tid)
  }

  /* The calling thread's interrupt status, cleared: java.lang.Thread's static
     interrupted(), which is what a program uses to poll the flag without an
     exception. */
  public static boolean interrupted() {
    return Thread.clearInterrupted0()
  }

  /* Daemon status, which decides whether the process waits for this thread.
     Java only allows it before start(); after that the thread's life is the
     runtime's business, and so is the count. */
  public final void setDaemon(boolean on) {
    /* java.lang.Thread refuses this for a virtual thread, and so does this:
       a virtual thread is a user thread the process waits for, and making it
       a daemon would be a promise the runtime's exit accounting does not
       keep. */
    if (this.virtual) {
      throw new UnsupportedOperationException("not supported for virtual threads")
    }
    if (this.started) {
      throw new IllegalThreadStateException("thread already started")
    }
    this.daemon = on
  }

  public final boolean isDaemon() {
    return this.daemon
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------------- ThreadLocal storage

  /* The map get()/set()/remove() below work on, made on first use. Package
     private: it is this file's business, not the program's. */
  HashMap<Object, Object> localMap() {
    if (this.locals == null) {
      this.locals = new HashMap<Object, Object>()
    }
    return this.locals
  }

  // ----------------------------------------------------------------- statics

  /* Give the processor up. Java's Thread.yield is a hint with no guarantee, and
     this is the same hint: the operating system chooses what runs next. */
  public static native void yield()

  /* Sleep for at least this many milliseconds. A negative duration is an
     IllegalArgumentException, as it is in Java; sleep(0) yields. An interrupt
     ends the sleep early with InterruptedException. */
  public static void sleep(long millis) {
    if (Thread.sleep0(millis)) {
      throw new InterruptedException()
    }
  }

  /* The Thread object of the calling thread. Inside a started thread that is the
     object start() was called on; in the main thread it is an object this makes
     the first time it is asked for, named "main" and numbered 1, so that the two
     threads a program is most likely to ask about behave alike. */
  private static Thread mainThread() {
    if (Thread.mainOf == null) {
      Thread t = new Thread()
      // the runtime already knows this thread: it is the one that has always
      // been there, and its id is 1
      t.tid = Thread.currentId0()
      t.name = "main"
      t.started = true
      Thread.bind0(t)
      Thread.mainOf = t
    }
    return Thread.mainOf
  }

  public static Thread currentThread() {
    Thread t = (Thread) Thread.current0()
    return (t != null) ? t : Thread.mainThread()
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------- virtual threads

  /* Start a virtual thread that runs task. Shorthand for
     ofVirtual().start(task), as in java.lang.Thread. */
  public static Thread startVirtualThread(Runnable task) {
    if (task == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("task")
    }
    return Thread.ofVirtual().start(task)
  }

  /* A builder for a virtual thread: name(String) sets its name and
     start(Runnable) starts it, which is the part of Java's
     Thread.Builder.OfVirtual a program uses. */
  public static Builder ofVirtual() {
    return new Builder()
  }

  static class Builder {
    private String vname

    public Builder name(String name) {
      if (name == null) {
        throw new NullPointerException("name")
      }
      this.vname = name
      return this
    }

    public Thread start(Runnable task) {
      if (task == null) {
        throw new NullPointerException("task")
      }
      Thread t = new Thread(task, this.vname)
      t.virtual = true
      /* A virtual thread is counted as a user thread: the process ends when
         the last one does, which is what this runtime's exit accounting
         already means by "non-daemon". It is not settable (setDaemon refuses),
         and startVirtual0 does not take the flag. */
      t.daemon = false
      t.start()
      return t
    }
  }
}

/* java.lang.ThreadLocal, one value per thread.
 *
 * The storage is the Thread object's own map (the `locals` field above), keyed
 * by this object. Java's ThreadLocal is the same design -- a map on the thread,
 * not a map on the ThreadLocal -- and the reason is the one that matters here:
 * a thread's values must not be visible to another thread and must not outlive
 * it. Keying by the ThreadLocal instance is identity, because ThreadLocal does
 * not override equals/hashCode; two different ThreadLocals are two keys, and
 * the same ThreadLocal seen from two threads finds two different maps.
 *
 * withInitial's supplier runs once per thread, on that thread's first get(), and
 * a value that was set() explicitly (including null) is not replaced by it.
 * remove() drops the entry, so the next get() calls the supplier again.
 *
 * One difference from the JDK worth knowing: Java's map has weak keys, so a
 * ThreadLocal that goes out of scope can be collected while the thread lives.
 * This one has strong keys -- there is no WeakReference in this library -- so a
 * ThreadLocal whose entry is never removed stays reachable from every thread
 * that read it, for as long as those threads live. A long-lived worker that
 * creates ThreadLocals per task should remove() them, which is the advice
 * Java's documentation gives too. */
class ThreadLocal<T> {
  private Supplier<? extends T> initial

  public ThreadLocal() {
  }

  private ThreadLocal(Supplier<? extends T> initial) {
    this.initial = initial
  }

  public static <S> ThreadLocal<S> withInitial(Supplier<? extends S> supplier) {
    return new ThreadLocal<S>(supplier)
  }

  public T get() {
    Thread t = Thread.currentThread()
    HashMap<Object, Object> map = t.localMap()
    if (map.containsKey(this)) {
      return (T) map.get(this)
    }
    if (this.initial == null) {
      return null
    }
    T value = this.initial.get()
    map.put(this, value)
    return value
  }

  public void set(T value) {
    Thread.currentThread().localMap().put(this, value)
  }

  public void remove() {
    Thread t = Thread.currentThread()
    if (t.locals != null) {
      t.locals.remove(this)
    }
  }
}
